{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":272503,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"144156b0-b1fb-4916-8049-b65feb32d0ef","total_cost_usd":1.2162444999999997,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":65994,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"output_tokens":20047,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":65994,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":20047,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"cache_creation_input_tokens":65994,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":65994},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":46526,"outputTokens":22,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.046636,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":20047,"cacheReadInputTokens":16967,"cacheCreationInputTokens":65994,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.1696084999999998,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"playtester\",\n  \"verdict\": \"targets_missed\",\n  \"summary\": \"Flown, this configuration never reaches orbit — all three ascents crash with the tank empty — and every descent placed in-band lands soft on a single pass, so the 2–4 pass aerobrake optimum, the mass-greed penalty and the rising-with-altitude return leg all fail as measured rather than as argued.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ascent_unreachable_all_bands\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"claim\": \"game_params balance_notes assert ve = 11000/4.6 = 2391 m/s, base dv = 2391*ln(1520/900) = 1254 m/s against a ~950 m/s ascent, so 'a base ship reaches the cheap band and comes home with reserve', with fuel_tank t1 as the gate that opens the low band.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The verification sweep flew all three ascents and reached none of them: suborbital (100 km), low (180 km) and high (295 km) all report reached=false, why='crashed on ascent', fuel_remaining_kg 0, fuel_margin_pct 0. Across the 2916-config exploration grid, bands_reachable is satisfied by 60.5% of worlds and fuel_margin_sane by only 19.5% — the scarcest target in the grid after unstaged survivability, so the dv budget is genuinely tight across the whole space, not just here.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"single_pass_always_cheapest\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"ablation.optimal_skims is 1/1/2 (suborbital/low/high) and the cost_curve minima sit at index 1, 1 and 2; the GDD requires that 'the cheapest descent is 2-4 committed passes, never a single plunge and never feathering'.\",\n      \"measured\": \"cheapest_pass_count is 1 in all nine band x load descents, and ablation rises monotonically with pass count in every one of them. Low band empty: 10.4, 13.9, 15.0, 15.8, 16.2, 16.9, 17.4, 17.8, 18.3, 18.9, 19.5 ... 22.2% at 16 passes. High band full: 11.7, 17.6, 18.9, 19.9 ... 29.4% at 23 passes. Suborbital empty: 11.9, 13.7, 14.5 ... 16.8% at 7. The anti-feathering half of the rule holds hard — the twentieth pass is always the most expensive — but the single plunge is the optimum everywhere, which is the exact failure §4.5 risk 1 predicted.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"full_hold_costs_nothing\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.5\",\n      \"claim\": \"2.3.5 states a full hold roughly doubles the rocket's mass at starting storage size; landing.descent_speed_full_hold_ms asserts 4.8 m/s, 'near but under the 5 m/s line'; 2.3.7 makes mass the mechanism by which difficulty scales with success.\",\n      \"measured\": \"cargo.kg_per_slot 16.23, full_hold_kg 97.4, full_hold_mass_ratio 1.11 — an 11% mass swing, not 100%. Touchdown speed is 3.39 m/s empty, 3.49 half hold, 3.57 full hold, identical in all three bands: a 5.3% spread, 29% under the soft-landing line, and no load in any band produces a hard landing. Ablation moves 11.9 -> 13.5% (suborbital) and 10.4 -> 11.8% (low) between empty and full. The greed decision has no measurable consequence anywhere in the shipping slice. The exploration grid satisfies greed_costs_something in 46.9% of worlds, so worlds where it bites do exist — but the controlling quantity is kg per cargo slot, which lives in the debris catalog, not in these params.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"skim_cooling_weaker_and_backwards_by_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"ablation.skim_heat_multiplier [1.0, 0.93, 0.89, 0.88] is applied to every band, with skim_peak 32, and the balance note claims the high band 'saves 5.6 points (11.6%) by skimming twice' while suborbital barely benefits.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Measured multipliers invert the intended shape: suborbital [1, 0.951, 0.897, 0.684], low [1, 0.977, 0.955, 0.934], high [1, 0.984, 0.968, 0.954]. Skimming cools most in the forgiving band (down to 0.684 by three skims) and least in the band the design needs it for (0.954 in high, i.e. 1.5% per skim against an assumed 7%). Measured skim_peak_heat is 7 / 8.2 / 9.6 on the 0-100 bar against the params' assumed 32 — the skims the simulator can actually fly are about a quarter as deep as the ablation model prices them, which is why the fatigue toll wins.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"return_leg_does_not_get_harder_with_altitude\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"claim\": \"Plate cost at the optimum rises 20 / 30 / 43 percent across suborbital / low / high, so 'the return leg gets harder with altitude as 2.3.7 requires'; §4.5 risk 2 names curve inversion as the design's central bet.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Cheapest ablation empty: suborbital 11.9%, low 10.4%, high 10.6%. Full hold: 13.5%, 11.8%, 11.7%. The high band is the cheapest return in the game and the suborbital band the most expensive — flat to inverted, at roughly a quarter of the claimed magnitudes. Peak heat at each optimum is 65 / 62 / 62 empty and 69 / 65 / 65 full, so the shield never comes near its limit from any altitude. The mechanism is visible in the braking altitudes: the optimal descent from the high band commits at 43012 m against suborbital's 47711 m — with more energy to spend, a perfect pilot simply enters shallower and pays nothing for the altitude. difficulty_rises_with_band is satisfied by 32.1% of the grid, so worlds where it holds exist.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"no_world_satisfies_every_target\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"4.5\",\n      \"claim\": \"The design asks simultaneously that skimming meaningfully cools the entry (2.3.1 ablation curve) and that a shallow unstaged braking pass be survivable (2.3.1 'shallow braking passes before you stage').\",\n      \"measured\": \"Across 2916 configurations the best score is 7 of 8; nothing scores 8. skimming_cools_the_entry is satisfied by 45.1% and unstaged_pass_survivable by only 15.4%, and every one of the twelve top configs fails exactly one of the two. The 60 km / 9.81 m/s2 worlds cool well by skimming (multipliers 0.363-0.44) but peak at 478-502 heat unstaged — instantly fatal; the 160 km / 3 m/s2 worlds survive unstaged passes (peak 59-90) but skim-cool only to 0.888-0.945, which is the regime the crew's own world sits in. The two targets trade against each other through the ballistic coefficient: the top skimmers run beta 11-44 kg/m2, below the ~50 floor where air removes the whole orbit, while the unstaged survivors run 133-1333.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"unstaged_pass_hotter_than_priced\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"unstaged_heat_multiplier 3.0 is set so 'a shallow unstaged pass peaking near 30 on the bar stays clear of the 100 threshold where cargo damage begins'.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Measured shallow unstaged peak heat is 62 (suborbital) and 71 (low) — survivable, so the pre-staging braking technique exists, but at roughly twice the claimed peak and with 29-38 points of margin rather than 70. The high band was not flown unstaged. Given that only 15.4% of grid worlds keep this target at all, this margin is the thing most likely to be destroyed by any change made to fix the skim findings.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"descent_wall_clock_forces_time_warp\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"4.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"§4.1 defers time compression, 'mitigated by scoping the planet small enough that a two-band orbit period is under a minute', with warp returning as a required feature only if the Researcher's scaling makes periods long.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Simulated descent durations: suborbital direct 1.04 h, suborbital three-skim 3.09 h, low three-skim 3.41 h, high direct 1.22 h and high three-skim 4.15-4.29 h. Even the cheapest single-pass suborbital descent — the first thing a new player flies — is over an hour of simulated time. The deferral's own condition is met, so on-rails warp is a required feature for the shipping slice, not a cut.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"direct_entry_is_the_only_place_heat_still_bites\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"reentry.heat_capacity 100 with cargo damage beginning at 100% heat, one slot per 3 s, is the anxiety mechanism of the descent.\",\n      \"measured\": \"A direct committed entry at the deepest sampled depth peaks at 100 (suborbital), 131.5 (low) and 163.1 (high) — the low and high band plunges do exceed the bar and would eat cargo, and the band-to-band spread there (0.76 / 1.0 / 1.24 normalised to low) is close to the params' claimed heat_index of 83 / 100 / 119. But every descent the optimiser actually chose peaks at 62-74, and shallowing the committed entry by 36000 m drops suborbital peak from 100 to 74 and high from 163.1 to 93.8. Entry depth is the dominant lever by a wide margin over both skim count and cargo mass, and it is currently free.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"parachute_units_are_not_in_the_config\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.2\",\n      \"claim\": \"landing.parachute_drag_coefficient 172.6 is documented, in a prose balance note only, as CdA expressed in multiples of baseline reference_area_m2 3.5 — i.e. 604 m2 of effective canopy.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The simulator found no canopy-area field and reports under `inferred` that it solved for the area implied by descent_speed_full_hold_ms 4.8 at twice dry mass, getting 6.0 m2 — two orders of magnitude from the note's reading. Every absolute touchdown figure in the sweep (3.39 / 3.49 / 3.57 m/s) is therefore anchored to the crew's own 4.8 m/s claim rather than independent of it. §4.4 makes the single config file the contract; a unit that only exists in a prose note is exactly the ambiguity that constraint is meant to remove.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"proposed_changes\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"flight.fuel_capacity_kg\",\n      \"current\": 620,\n      \"proposed\": 900,\n      \"reason\": \"Raises base dv from ~1254 to ~1656 m/s so the suborbital ascent has margin for the gravity losses the flown profile actually pays; costs the sharpness of the fuel_tank t1 band gate, so the tank upgrade tiers should be re-priced upward with it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"flight.thrust_n\",\n      \"current\": 11000,\n      \"proposed\": 13500,\n      \"reason\": \"Restores liftoff TWR to 1.87 at the larger wet mass (1800 kg) instead of letting it fall to 1.53, which is where a slow gravity turn burns the new margin straight back off.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"flight.fuel_burn_kgs\",\n      \"current\": 4.6,\n      \"proposed\": 5.65,\n      \"reason\": \"Holds exhaust velocity at ~2390 m/s (Isp 244) alongside the thrust increase, so the engine stays a plausible scrapyard engine rather than quietly becoming a 299 s vacuum motor.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.cycle_toll_base_pct\",\n      \"current\": 0.53,\n      \"proposed\": 0.15,\n      \"reason\": \"The measured per-skim cooling is 1.5-4.9%, not the 7-12% the curve was priced against, so the thermal-cycling toll currently outweighs every skim's saving and makes one pass cheapest everywhere; the optimum must be re-measured after this change rather than asserted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.cycle_toll_growth\",\n      \"current\": 1.5,\n      \"proposed\": 1.15,\n      \"reason\": \"Keeps the geometric toll that correctly kills feathering — measured ablation still rises monotonically past 16 passes — while letting the second and third skim survive it; if the optimum still lands at 1 pass after both toll changes, take §4.5 risk 1's own fallback of a flat per-pass cost with a hard pass cap rather than nudging further.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.skim_heat_multiplier\",\n      \"current\": [1, 0.93, 0.89, 0.88],\n      \"proposed\": [1, 0.977, 0.955, 0.934],\n      \"reason\": \"Replaces the estimated array with the low-band measurement, which is the band the cost curve is normalised to; the params should price what the simulator flew, and the balance note already says to adopt the measurement if one exists.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.skim_peak\",\n      \"current\": 32,\n      \"proposed\": 9,\n      \"reason\": \"Measured skim peak heat is 7 / 8.2 / 9.6 across the three bands, so the model has been charging heat cost for a skim four times deeper than any flown one — this is the single largest source of the gap between the claimed and measured cost curves.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"baseline.bands[2].altitude_min_m\",\n      \"current\": 250000,\n      \"proposed\": 400000,\n      \"reason\": \"The (R+h) term is the only honest lever on band-to-band entry speed and is the Researcher's own first-listed fix; pushing the high band out restores the spread that a perfect pilot currently escapes by entering shallower, at the cost of a longer period that makes the time-warp requirement harder still.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"baseline.bands[2].altitude_max_m\",\n      \"current\": 340000,\n      \"proposed\": 520000,\n      \"reason\": \"Keeps the band's width proportional to the new floor so per-altitude speed still varies meaningfully inside it; the high band is post-semester, so the cost of moving it now is close to zero.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"flight.dry_mass_kg\",\n      \"current\": 900,\n      \"proposed\": 650,\n      \"reason\": \"The only in-params lever on the full-hold mass ratio, and it is not enough on its own — 1.11 becomes 1.16, staged beta falls 171 to 124 — so treat it as a partial move and fix cargo mass where it actually lives, in the debris catalog's kg per slot (measured 16.23, needs roughly 150).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"landing.parachute_area_m2\",\n      \"current\": null,\n      \"proposed\": 6.0,\n      \"reason\": \"Adds the field the simulator had to infer, so descent speed stops being circularly derived from the descent speed the params assert; if the intended canopy is really the 604 m2 in the balance note, state that instead and every touchdown figure in this sweep must be re-flown.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"confidence_notes\": [\n    \"The ascent leg is the thinnest thing in the sweep: it reports one boolean and three zeros per band, with no burn profile, no gravity-loss accounting and no indication of what guidance was flown. A point mass on a fixed pitch program can throw away several hundred m/s that a human would not. I still report it as blocking — nothing else in the run is a player-reachable state without it — but it is the first thing worth re-measuring, and the proposed fuel and thrust numbers are sized to give margin against an unknown loss rather than against a measured one.\",\n    \"The descents were flown from in-band initial conditions rather than from a flown ascent, so every descent finding stands independently of the ascent failure. The two halves of the sweep do not contaminate each other.\",\n    \"Absolute touchdown speeds are not an independent check. The simulator solved for canopy area from the params' own descent_speed_full_hold_ms 4.8 claim (see `inferred`), so 3.39 / 3.49 / 3.57 m/s inherit that assumption. The 1.11 mass ratio and the 5.3% empty-to-full spread are independent of it — the spread scales as sqrt(mass) whatever the canopy — so the greed finding survives even if the absolute figures are wrong.\",\n    \"The simulator flies a perfect pilot: no attitude error, so off_retrograde_penalty 1.0 is never exercised and every measured peak heat is a best case. By the params' own cosine rule a steady 20 degrees off retrograde adds about 6% heat, and a human holding retrograde through plasma will do worse than that. Read the 62-74 peak heats as a floor.\",\n    \"Nothing in either sweep exercises EVA, tethers, shear, latch jerk or the compactor. The magnet hold force, jerk coefficients and slot-cost claims in game_params are untested by me and I make no assertion about them either way.\",\n    \"The exploration grid is a coarse 4x3x3x3x3x3x3 lattice scoring booleans, so '45.1% satisfiable' describes that lattice, not a continuous probability — a world between grid points may well do better on both contested targets. What the grid does establish strongly is the absence of any 8/8 in 2916 configurations, and the clean split in the top twelve between skim-coolers with beta 11-44 and unstaged-survivors with beta 133-1333. That trade is a design statement, not a tuning gap, and I would not spend sweeps hunting for the world that has both.\",\n    \"Heat figures are on the simulator's own normalisation — empty ship, single pass, suborbital band, pegged at 100. Every heat number I quote is relative to that, including the direct-entry peaks above 100, and none of them is a watt.\",\n    \"I did not test whether the proposed toll changes actually move the optimum to 2-4 passes; with measured multipliers of 0.977 per skim the saving per skim is a few tenths of a percent of plate, and the toll has to fall below that to be beaten. If a re-flown sweep still puts the optimum at 1, that is the point to stop tuning and take the GDD's flat-cost-plus-pass-cap fallback.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":190812,"ttft_stream_ms":1748,"time_to_request_ms":214,"type":"result","duration_ms":270434,"uuid":"66893917-0807-4b34-83f7-a71aee23d7b0"}
